    "result_text": "All done. The full test suite is a single test class (`AcceptanceTest`); the helper files (`AcceptanceBase`, `AuditLogCapture`) have no `@Test` methods. The build is green.\n\n## Summary\n\nI replaced the string membership tier with a numeric `membershipLevel` (1\u20133):\n\n**`MembershipLevel.java`** (new, replaces `MembershipTier.java`) \u2014 computes the level from the owner's own fields: start at 1, `+1` when an email is present, `+1` when `namesakeCount == 0`, capped at 3. The old GOLD/SILVER/BRONZE string logic (including the household-size rule) is gone, per the spec.\n\n**`openapi.yml`** \u2014 swapped the `membershipTier` string schema for a `membershipLevel` integer field (`minimum: 1`, `maximum: 3`, read-only). The DTO is generated from this, so `OwnerDto` now carries `membershipLevel` and no longer has `membershipTier`.\n\n**`OwnerMapper.java`** \u2014 maps `membershipLevel` via `MembershipLevel.of(owner)`. Since the level no longer needs household lookups, I removed the now-unused `OwnerRepository` autowiring and updated the class Javadoc.\n\n**`AuditOwnerCreated.java`** \u2014 the create audit line now records `membershipLevel=...` (the thing that recorded the tier now records the level). You can see it in the run output: `AUDIT - owner created id=11 customerCode=TOW-LEV-0001 registrationDate=2026-08-17 membershipLevel=3`.\n\nTest result: `Tests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0` \u2014 `BUILD SUCCESS`. The new `coreNumericLevel` test (owner with email + unique name \u2192 `membershipLevel` = 3) passes.\n\nOne judgment call worth flagging: the spec's parenthetical implied the create audit line already recorded the tier, but it didn't \u2014 it logged only id/customerCode/registrationDate. I added `membershipLevel` to it to satisfy \"must record the level,\" since no test asserts its absence and it matches the spec's intent.",
